      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "callout_0",
      "quote": "Several systematic reviews and meta-analyses on diabetes and COVID19 prognosis have observed an approximately two- to threefold increased risk of mortality due to COVID-19 for people with diabetes compared with people without diabetes [6 - 14]. However, diabetes is a complex and heterogeneous disease and recent studies have found that there are differences in associations of specific phenotypes of diabetes with comorbidities and complications [15]. Regarding COVID-19, phenotypes related to more severe forms of diabetes, such as uncontrolled blood glucose, the presence of diabetes-related complications, a higher BMI, elevated biomarkers for liver damage and inflammation, are linked to early death, endotracheal intubations or admission to intensive care units (ICUs) [16 - 18]. However, some of the findings are still conflicting, imprecisely estimated or affected by risk of bias, such as confounding.",
      "reason": "The text describes the current state of knowledge regarding diabetes and COVID-19 prognosis, citing existing systematic reviews, and highlights important uncertainties such as conflicting findings and risk of bias."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "callout_0",
      "quote": "Thus, findings from single studies are difficult to translate to clinical practice. To provide the best available evidence for the identification of risk phenotypes of diabetes in association with COVID-19 severity and death, a systematic review and meta-analysis is needed that summarises the findings, reveals more robust estimates, considers risk of bias and evaluates the certainty of evidence.",
      "reason": "The text explicitly explains the importance and need for the review to translate findings to clinical practice and provide robust evidence by summarizing findings and evaluating certainty."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "callout_0",
      "quote": "Several systematic reviews and meta-analyses on diabetes and COVID19 prognosis have observed an approximately two- to threefold increased risk of mortality due to COVID-19 for people with diabetes compared with people without diabetes [6 - 14]. However, diabetes is a complex and heterogeneous disease and recent studies have found that there are differences in associations of specific phenotypes of diabetes with comorbidities and complications [15]. Regarding COVID-19, phenotypes related to more severe forms of diabetes... are linked to early death... However, some of the findings are still conflicting, imprecisely estimated or affected by risk of bias, such as confounding. Thus, findings from single studies are difficult to translate to clinical practice. To provide the best available evidence for the identification of risk phenotypes of diabetes in association with COVID-19 severity and death, a systematic review and meta-analysis is needed that summarises the findings, reveals more robust estimates, considers risk of bias and evaluates the certainty of evidence.",
      "reason": "The text identifies and cites previous systematic reviews [6-14] and explains the need for the current review to address conflicts, imprecision, and risk of bias in existing literature."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review focuses on observational studies investigating risk phenotypes (prognostic factors), not intervention effects, so a mechanism of action is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review focuses on observational studies investigating risk phenotypes (prognostic factors), not complex interventions, so a logic model is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 95
